y have to wait or the  end organ damage which is caused by that  uncontrolled immune response you can  hopefully start to earlier detect  indications of the immune response  through elevated levels of procalcitonin  now dr. Hayden are you planning on  moving this to the roche analyzers to  improve t18 I would say it is twofold  the first is definitely to improve the  turnaround time the second really is  going to be the operational savings  we're in the midst of getting ready for  our c AP inspection window and so i'm  thinking carefully about all of our  potential vulnerabilities for citations  and it's amazing if you just go through  the cap checklist how many items are  there for each instrument you have in  the lab and so a chance to do platform  consolidation to have a single analyzer  which can do some of these analytes like  the i/o PTH the troponin procalcitonin  etc saves us a substantial amount of  work and then I only have to Train folks  on a single in  ermand I only have to have a single  interface yeah so it is the turn around  time improvement but I suspect we're  going to realize some very helpful  operational savings in the ROC curves  you showed for a better SN and SP with  PCT over lactate other what time points  for PCT measurement were used to  diagnose step so that's a good question  I'm actually not  I don't recall the time points used so  PCT is going to rise up relatively  quickly you know within six hours they  say so it would be early time point this  would not be days later and I would have  to look my suspicion is most of the  trials I've looked at which have been  prospective has done it at what we would  call time zero so time suspicions of  sepsis but there are certainly been some  retrospective looks which have used  whatever time it was ordered so I'm  sorry I'm not not sure dr. Hayden  are there different manufacturers that  offer PCT PCT testing oh yes absolutely  certainly there are there are other  manufacturers who offer there 